description:
The Disability Representative and Carer Organisation (DRCO) Forum meets to discuss improvements to, and the experiences of participants in, the National Disability Insurance Scheme (NDIS) (Participants).
DRCO Forum members assist the National Disability Insurance Agency (Agency) to perform its functions, meet its obligations, and to achieve its overarching objectives in a manner that is consistent with the National Disability Insurance Scheme Act 2013 (Cth), including
• provision of expert advice to improve the implementation of the NDIS;
• collection and analysis of the systemic voice of people with disability and their family/supporters across a diverse range of disability types and communities on key NDIS reforms;
• participating in and supporting all forms of consultation and engagement with the Agency;
• undertaking outreach, engagement, and consultation with Participants; and
• advising on NDIS strategy or solution designs.

The services will comprise of two complementary but distinct service streams, with separate delivery, financial management and performance reporting arrangements for each program. DFAT is seeking to secure a flexible, responsive and participant-centered provider to:
• deliver accessible information and practical support to participants from eligible Pacific countries and Timor-Leste on ballot, visa, employment, pre-departure and settlement stages to make informed decisions, according to the scope of each service stream;
• connect participants with suitable and timely employment opportunities, recognising that a formal job offer is a PEV visa requirement while FMP employment support is focused on successful settlement and economic participation;
• deliver enhanced, culturally grounded offshore (in Tuvalu) and onshore (in Australia) support for FMP participants consistent with mobility with dignity and the bilateral significance of the Falepili Union Treaty;
• collect and report regular, timely, high-quality, quantitative and qualitative disaggregated program and participant data to support performance monitoring, decision-making and continuous improvement;
• adapt delivery to changing allocations, countries, participant needs and level of demand, policy settings and settlement patterns; and
• support FMP participants and the Tuvaluan diaspora in Australia to maintain ongoing connections to Tuvalu, including cultural identity, language, community networks and links to country, consistent with the commitments of the Falepili Union Treaty.

The Invitation to provide Day Procedure Centre Services to the Veteran Community seeks to ensure that the veteran and Defence Force communities, and their treating doctors, continue to have wide access to high quality stand-alone Day Procedure Centre services.
Eligible providers are invited to submit an Invitation Response Proforma and apply to contract with DVA through a new, simplified arrangement. The key features of the new arrangements are:
an overall objective of maximising the numbers of participating providers, to achieve wide access to DPCs for the veteran community;
there is no formal comparison of responses against each other;
providers are required to submit their fee tables and acknowledge their compliance with the terms and conditions of this Invitation;
providers declare their ability to comply with DVA’s terms and conditions of the DPC Services Agreement;
providers can submit a response and apply to enter into the DPC Services Agreement at any time while this Invitation is available on AusTender;
the Agreement will continue until either party ends the arrangements; and
there is a standard DPC Services Agreement with negotiation only on fees to ensure value for money.

Joint Approach to Market – Office Accommodation in Hobart, Tasmania.
Services Australia, acting on behalf of the Commonwealth and the Participating Entities (the Commonwealth), invites Tenderers to submit Proposals for the provision of contemporary, fit-for-purpose office accommodation in Hobart, Tasmania.
Proposals are invited for accommodation solutions that:
• are located within the Permitted Geographic Area identified in the RFP Statement of Requirement;
• provide approximately 10,000 to 14,400 square metres of Net Lettable Area;
• provide flexibility to increase or decrease the Premises by 15%;
• desirably include a ground-floor component with a minimum area of approximately 830 square metres;
• provide contiguous floor plates within the building footprint;
• provide PCA A-grade accommodation or better;
• comprise an existing building ready for use, an existing building requiring refurbishment, or a new development opportunity; and
• support the Commonwealth’s target Lease Commencement Date of 1 July 2031, or an earlier date by mutual agreement.
A minimum of 43 car parking bays is highly desirable, comprising 41 secure and covered bays, with at least 25% of spaces provided with electric vehicle charging, and two accessible bays at ground level close to the entrance.
The Commonwealth is seeking an initial lease(s) term of 10 years, with two options to renew of three years each.
The Commonwealth will consider Tenderer-delivered, integrated and Commonwealth-delivered fit-out solutions, as further detailed in the RFP Documents.

The Department of Defence is seeking an experienced organisation to act as a national sales partner for the management and conduct of online auction-based sales of surplus Commonwealth assets.
Defence intends to establish a long-term contractual arrangement with a provider capable of delivering integrated asset sales services across Australia.
Services may include:
- collection of assets from nominated locations;
- storage and handling of assets prior to sale;
- asset cataloguing and sale preparation activities;
- sales and marketing advice;
- marketing and promotion of assets for sale;
- operation of online auction services;
- buyer and customer management;
- sale transaction administration; and
- reporting and performance management.
Assets may include vehicles, plant and equipment, machinery, containers, watercraft, tools, inventory and other surplus Commonwealth assets.
Defence is seeking responses from organisations with demonstrated capability in auction-based asset sales services and the capacity to deliver services on a national basis.
Further details of the requirement are contained in the request documentation.

publicQuestions.question:
1. Carpet extent – check-in and screening areas

Council’s response of 9 November states that the existing check-in baggage drop-off area requires no works “with the exception of carpet replacement”. The response of 23 November states that carpet replacement is “limited to the Departure Lounge only”. Can Council please confirm whether new carpet tiles are required to the following areas:

a. the check-in area, including behind the check-in counters;

b. the security screening area and screening line-up;

c. the main entry;

d. the security room.

2. Extent of the Arrival Lounge (terrazzo)

Council’s response of 3 November confirms terrazzo is to be provided “in the Arrival Lounge only”. Can Council please confirm the extent of the Arrival Lounge on Drawing CD2? Specifically, does it include:

a. the whole Arrivals Baggage Collection area between Grids 7 and 8, up to the new glazed wall at Grid 7;

b. the customer side of the Hire Car Desk;

c. the exit area to the north auto door?

3. Floor finishes not nominated

The Finishes Schedule does not nominate a floor finish for the following areas. Can Council please confirm whether it has a preferred finish for each, or whether they are to be selected by the Contractor under Design Requirements Section 20 and submitted for approval:

a. the lobby outside the new accessible, male and female toilets (under the “Plant Over” notation on CD2);

b. the staff side of the Hire Car Desk;

c. the new external Baggage Drop Off area under the new awning between Grids 8 and 9. For this area, can Council please also advise the design loads for the slab and pavement, including any baggage tug and trolley traffic?

4. Entrance matting

The tender documents do not include entrance matting. Can Council please confirm whether recessed entrance matting is required at external entry and exit doors (main entry, arrivals exit, airside arrivals door and departure gates)? If so, does Council have a preferred product or extent?

5. Fire safety certification

Council’s response of 18 November advised that the existing fire safety certification would be provided once finalised. Can Council please confirm whether it will be issued before tender close? If not, can tenderers assume the existing Fire Safety Schedule dated 7 July 2025 is current?

6. Terrazzo alternative (optional)

Would Council consider a large-format porcelain tile as a separately priced alternative to poured terrazzo in the Arrival Lounge? The poured terrazzo would remain the conforming offer.

description:
The purpose of this Expression of Interest (EOI) is to enable Applicants to demonstrate their capability, relevant experience, delivery approach, and ability to achieve sustainability and social procurement outcomes required for the delivery of the Project.

The EOI Documents provide details of Procurement Process, EOI Conditions, the Evaluation Process, the Principal's objectives, and key details that the Applicant needs to know to submit a conforming EOI Submission. This EOI submission will be used to establish a shortlist of Tenderers to progress to the next phase of the procurement process.

The Applicant will need to address the physical construction of the facility, progressing from bulk earthworks through to the completed building. This includes:

1. site preparation and bulk earthworks;

2. substructure elements, including foundations and ground slab;

3. superstructure, including concrete and primary framing elements;

4. façade and roofing;

5. management of residual contamination issues in accordance with the Site Management Plan (SMP) including design mitigations and ongoing monitoring/reporting; and

6. building services works.

Fit out and finishing works will follow, encompassing building fit out and associated services, together with the delivery of on grade carparking, service areas and landscaping.

Finally, the Applicant will be responsible for building commissioning and certification including DETSI approvals, and for the management of project sustainability requirements throughout Delivery.

vendorPanelDetails.background:
The proposed Moreton Bay Indoor Sports Centre (MBISC) has been identified as a host venue for the 2032 Brisbane Olympic and Paralympic Games and will be located within Moreton Bay Central which is a new mixed-use precinct being developed by City of Moreton Bay on the site of a former paper mill in Petrie.

MBISC addresses an identified service need and provides a fit-for-purpose facility that delivers a significant uplift in the number of indoor sports courts in Moreton Bay. The venue will enhance the local area, providing economic opportunities and enhanced amenity to a rapidly growing area of south-east Queensland.

MBISC will help to provide the following benefits:

1. increased local sporting opportunities and pathways;

2. healthier lifestyles and choices, providing improved local livability;

3. more cohesive, inclusive and safe community;

4. business attraction and local employment opportunities; and

5. positive Games experience for participants and spectators.

The MBISC will be owned and operated by City of Moreton Bay (CMB), delivering a lasting legacy for the region through enhanced tourism, economic development, and increased opportunities for community engagement and active recreation.

