description:
Tenders are invited for from interested parties to establish a café within the Aura Lagoon and Parklands Caloundra South precinct. Operations will commence following execution of the lease, and as soon as the lease area has been accepted by Council for Occupation. This is currently anticipated for April 2027.

The lettable area is leased as a cold shell, with the successful lessee require to undertake all internal design and fit-out obligations.

- Kiosk Area- 50m2

- Outdoor seating area - to be confirmed once construction completed and Survey plan completed.

The successful operator is required to provide café services that are reflective of the unique lagoon environment.

Café services will be required to conform as follows:

- Seven (7) day a week operation;

- Operations during daylight hours;

- Substantial Dine in and take away menu options;

- Presents a menu that caters to varying dietary requirements;

- An operation that looks to utilise sustainable practices in its food preparation, use and waste processes; and

- Menu selection and prices that suits the broad demographics of visitors to the lagoon precinct.

Following selection of a preferred Respondent, Council intends to enter into a Heads of Agreement prior to finalisation of the Lease Agreement. Execution of the Heads of Agreement will be a mandatory requirement of the procurement process and respondents should ensure they have the necessary authority and resources available to negotiate and execute the Heads of Agreement within the timeframe nominated by Council.

publicQuestions.answer:
Hi,

The utility relocations were originally intended to be delivered by the developer as part of the adjoining development works. Accordingly, Council has not been involved in, and is not aware of, any approved utility relocation designs, submissions or authority approvals associated with these works, including any ASP design.

Council will continue to liaise with the developer to seek any available background information. However, the developer has advised that the provided designs were prepared several years ago and that project-specific requirements, including confirmation of service relocation extents and locations, would need to be further developed during detailed design with a contractor onboard. To date, no additional information has/can be provided by the developer.

Accordingly, Tenderers are to allow for all utility relocation works required to deliver Optional Stages 2 and 3, including but not limited to:

	• Investigation and confirmation of existing services;

	• Design development and coordination with utility authorities and stakeholders;

	• Identification and nomination of suitable relocated service alignments and locations;

	• Preparation and approval of any required utility relocation designs, including ASP requirements where applicable; and

	• Construction, commissioning, make-good and reinstatement works necessary to complete the utility relocations.

Tenderers should make their own allowances for all utility relocation works necessary to facilitate delivery of Optional Stages 2 and 3.

description:
Livingstone Shire Council is inviting tenders from suitably qualified and experienced building contractors, shed construction specialists and design and construct contractors to deliver Stage 1 of the Stevenson Park Redevelopment at the Yeppoon Showgrounds, Morris Street, Yeppoon.

The project will provide new accommodation for three local community organisations and prepare the site for the future Stevenson Park Community Centre precinct.

The scope of works includes:

•	Detailed design, engineering certification and relevant statutory approvals;

•	Supply and construction of two new steel-framed community tenancy sheds, including covered verandahs;

•	Associated electrical, plumbing, drainage and external works;

•	Coordination of community tenant relocation;

•	Partial demolition of the Gus Moore Pavilion, including construction of a new external wall to the retained Yeppoon Little Theatre;

•	Demolition of the Ken Landsberg Pavilion and associated infrastructure;

•	Site remediation, earthworks and reinstatement; and

•	Testing, commissioning, certification and handover.

Council welcomes submissions from experienced shed builders and building contractors with access to appropriately qualified engineering professionals and licensed plumbing, electrical, concreting and demolition subcontractors.

The Works will be delivered under an AS 4300-1995 Design and Construct Contract, with Council providing the concept design.

Construction activities must be carefully coordinated to maintain safe public access and minimise disruption to existing community facilities, sporting activities and events.

